From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Date: Fri, 14 Aug 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] tr_lf_cr: take a non-const string

atinout builds with -Werror and stops on:

    atinout.c: In function 'tr_lf_cr':
    atinout.c:84:11: error: assignment discards 'const' qualifier from pointer
                            target type [-Werror=discarded-qualifiers]
       84 |         p = strchr(s, '\n');

C23 makes strchr() and friends qualifier-preserving (N3020), so with GCC 15
defaulting to -std=gnu23 and a glibc that implements it, strchr() on a
const char * now returns a const char * instead of a char *.

The declaration was wrong to begin with: tr_lf_cr() writes through the pointer
it is given ("*p = '\r'"), so taking it as const char * was casting the
qualifier away regardless of what strchr() returned. Its only caller passes
"line", which is already a plain char *, so make the parameter match what the
function actually does.

Upstream-Status: Pending
Signed-off-by: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
---
 atinout.c | 2 +-
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)

--- a/atinout.c
+++ b/atinout.c
@@ -78,7 +78,7 @@
 }
 
 /* Replace '\n' with '\r', aka `tr '\012' '\015'` */
-static bool tr_lf_cr(const char *s)
+static bool tr_lf_cr(char *s)
 {
 	char *p;
 	p = strchr(s, '\n');
